[{"sources":[{"title":"The Lotus Sutra","author":"Anonymous"},{"title":"Bodhicaryavatara","author":"Shantideva"},{"title":"The Heart Sutra","author":"Anonymous"}],"relatedEntities":{"deities":[{"id":"buddhist_deity_avalokiteshvara","name":"Avalokiteshvara","relationship":"associated"},{"id":"buddhist_deity_manjushri","name":"Manjushri","relationship":"associated"}]},"category":"spiritual","shortDescription":"Bodhisattvas are enlightened beings who have postponed their own nirvana to help others achieve enlightenment. They embody compassion and wisdom, striving to liberate all sentient beings from suffering.","relatedConcepts":["buddhist_concept_prajna","buddhist_concept_karuna","buddhist_concept_bodhicitta"],"_uploadedAt":"2026-01-18T12:44:36.261Z","name":"Bodhisattvas","_uploadedBy":"push-to-firebase.js","mythology":"buddhist","id":"bodhisattvas","description":"In Buddhism, a Bodhisattva (Sanskrit: बोधिसत्त्व; Pali: बोधिसत्त) is an enlightened being who, out of compassion, has generated Bodhicitta, a spontaneous wish and a compassionate mind to attain Buddhahood for the benefit of all sentient beings. Bodhisattvas are a central figure in Mahayana Buddhism, where the path of the Bodhisattva is considered the ideal path to enlightenment. They vow to remain in the cycle of rebirth until all beings are liberated from suffering, embodying the virtues of wisdom (prajna) and compassion (karuna). The concept of the Bodhisattva is rooted in the idea that enlightenment is not merely a personal achievement but a universal responsibility. They cultivate the Six Perfections (Paramitas): Generosity (Dana), Morality (Sila), Patience (Kshanti), Effort (Virya), Concentration (Dhyana), and Wisdom (Prajna). Some famous Bodhisattvas include Avalokiteshvara (the embodiment of compassion), Manjushri (the embodiment of wisdom), and Vajrapani (the embodiment of power). Different Buddhist traditions emphasize different aspects of the Bodhisattva path, but the core principle remains the same: to attain Buddhahood for the sake of all beings. The Bodhisattva ideal is a cornerstone of Mahayana Buddhist ethics and practice, inspiring countless individuals to dedicate their lives to the service of others and the pursuit of enlightenment.","type":"mythology","_backlinks":[{"ref":"places/borobudur","name":"Borobudur","collection":"places","domain":"mythology","relationship":"related"}]},{"relatedMythologies":["hindu","chinese","japanese"],"_modified":"2025-12-27T07:07:21.147Z","description":"Buddhist spiritual tradition encompassing the path to enlightenment, bodhisattvas like Avalokiteshvara, the Wheel of Dharma, and liberation from samsara.","displayName":"Buddhist Tradition","renderConfig":{"showRelatedMythologies":true,"showCategoryGrid":true,"theme":"dharmic","enableCorpusSearch":true},"timePeriod":{"era":"Ancient to Modern","end":"Present","start":"563 BCE"},"status":"active","categories":{"cosmology":{"enabled":true,"count":9},"heroes":{"enabled":true,"count":5},"magic":{"count":0,"enabled":true},"concepts":{"enabled":true,"count":2},"texts":{"count":0,"enabled":true},"herbs":{"enabled":true,"count":1},"places":{"count":0,"enabled":true},"items":{"enabled":true,"count":0},"deities":{"enabled":true,"count":16},"symbols":{"enabled":true,"count":0},"rituals":{"count":2,"enabled":true},"creatures":{"enabled":true,"count":1},"events":{"count":0,"enabled":true}},"icon":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 64 64\">\n  <circle cx=\"32\" cy=\"32\" r=\"28.8\" fill=\"#9370DB\" opacity=\"0.2\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"32\" cy=\"32\" r=\"19.2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#9370DB\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,5\"/>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"44.8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"22.4\" fill=\"#9370DB\">✨</text>\n</svg>","featured":true,"order":5,"mythology":"buddhist","color":"#51cf66","iconType":"svg","type":"mythology","lastUpdated":"2025-12-26T03:59:25.038Z","id":"buddhist","name":"Buddhist Tradition","stats":{"heroCount":5,"totalEntities":36,"domainCount":6,"archetypeCount":0,"deityCount":8,"creatureCount":1},"createdAt":"2025-12-25T11:04:39.046Z","metadata":{"createdAt":"2025-12-13T02:44:36.725Z","verified":false,"lastUpdatedBy":"agent6-mythology-fixer","source":"html_parser","lastUpdateReason":"Added type field and expanded description","createdBy":"system","deityCount":8,"updatedAt":"2025-12-27T07:06:18.473Z"},"culturalTags":["Dharmic","Asian","Contemplative"],"sections":["spiritual_path","general","general","cosmology","cosmology","general","general","general","general","creatures","general","herbs","herbs","rituals","rituals","general","rituals","general","texts","symbols","general","general","herbs","general","general","general","general","general","general"],"heroTitle":"The Path to Enlightenment & the Wheel of Dharma","longDescription":"Walk the Eightfold Path and discover the teachings of the Buddha. From Avalokiteshvara's compassion to the realms of samsara, explore the Dharma."},{"relatedConcepts":["buddhist_concept_prajna","buddhist_concept_upaya"],"relatedEntities":{"deities":[{"id":"buddhist_deity_avalokiteshvara","name":"Avalokiteshvara","relationship":"associated"},{"id":"buddhist_deity_vajrapani","name":"Vajrapani","relationship":"associated"},{"id":"buddhist_deity_vairochana","name":"Vairochana","relationship":"associated"}]},"_uploadedBy":"push-to-firebase.js","type":"mythology","category":"spiritual","shortDescription":"Mahayana deities are enlightened beings, Bodhisattvas and Buddhas, who play a central role in the Mahayana Buddhist pantheon, offering guidance and assistance to practitioners on the path to enlightenment.","id":"mahayana_deities","description":"Mahayana Buddhism encompasses a vast pantheon of deities, representing different aspects of enlightenment, compassion, and wisdom. These deities are not merely figures of worship but are seen as embodiments of specific qualities and principles. They serve as archetypes for practitioners to emulate and as sources of inspiration and refuge. The Mahayana pantheon includes Buddhas, such as Amitabha (associated with the Pure Land) and Vairochana (the cosmic Buddha), and Bodhisattvas, such as Avalokiteshvara (the embodiment of compassion), Manjushri (the embodiment of wisdom), and Tara (a goddess of compassion and protection). These deities are often depicted in elaborate iconography, with specific mudras (hand gestures), colors, and attributes that symbolize their unique qualities and functions. The worship of Mahayana deities often involves visualization practices, mantras, and offerings, aimed at cultivating the qualities they represent within oneself and receiving their blessings. The concept of skillful means (upaya) is central to understanding the role of these deities, as they are seen as manifesting in various forms to guide beings according to their individual needs and capacities. The emphasis on accessibility and compassion in Mahayana Buddhism has led to the widespread veneration of these deities, making them integral to the spiritual lives of millions of practitioners worldwide. They are seen as active participants in the world, working tirelessly to alleviate suffering and guide beings towards enlightenment.","name":"Mahayana deities","sources":[{"title":"The Princeton Dictionary of Buddhism","author":"Robert E. Buswell Jr. and Donald S. Lopez Jr."},{"title":"Buddhism: A Very Short Introduction","author":"Damien Keown"},{"title":"Living Zen","author":"Robert Aitken"}],"mythology":"buddhist","_uploadedAt":"2026-01-18T12:44:36.261Z"},{"description":"A comprehensive collection of 49 entities from Buddhist mythology. This hub serves as a navigation index for exploring all related deities, heroes, creatures, items, and more.","icon":"☸️","relatedEntities":{"cosmology":[{"id":"buddhist_afterlife","name":"Bardo, Rebirth & Nirvana\"","type":"cosmology","relationship":"associated"},{"id":"buddhist_creation","name":"Interdependent Origination","type":"cosmology","relationship":"associated